<!-- One sample document in plain semantic HTML inside a text-display="prose"
     region and nothing else, so what renders is exactly what a marked
     artifact region gets. -->
<main text-display="prose">
<header>
  <h1>Quarterly reliability report</h1>
  <p>Where the platform held, where it strained, and what we change next quarter.</p>
</header>

<p>Uptime landed at <code>99.94%</code> across the fleet, with the two visible incidents both traced to the <a href="#">connection pool exhaustion</a> first flagged in March. The pool was resized and the alert threshold moved ahead of saturation rather than at it.</p>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>Three findings shaped the quarter:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Retry storms amplified the April outage — clients retried in lockstep after the first failure.</li>
  <li>The staging environment drifted from production kernels, hiding a scheduler regression.</li>
  <li>On-call escalations resolved fastest when the runbook linked the exact dashboard.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote>
  <p>The pool did not fail loudly. It failed by getting slow, and slow looks like everything else until you graph it.</p>
</blockquote>

<h3>The fix, in order</h3>

<ol>
  <li>Jittered exponential backoff in every client.</li>
  <li>Kernel parity checks in the deploy pipeline.</li>
  <li>Dashboards linked from each runbook step.</li>
</ol>

<pre><code>pool:
  max_connections: 512
  saturation_alert: 0.8</code></pre>

<h2>The numbers</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Month</th><th>Uptime</th><th>Incidents</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>April</td><td>99.87%</td><td>2</td></tr>
    <tr><td>May</td><td>99.98%</td><td>0</td></tr>
    <tr><td>June</td><td>99.97%</td><td>0</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<hr />

<p>Full incident writeups live in the ops channel; press <kbd>g</kbd> then <kbd>r</kbd> to jump there.</p>
</main>
